\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Stabilizer labels and helicity}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In the finite semidirect product of
\ref{fdip-016F}, one momentum orbit and one irreducible type of the
whole stabilizer specify an irreducible module.
\citet{§8.2, Proposition 25, pp. 62-63}{serre1977linear}
describes this construction over the complex numbers as the method
of little groups. A degree in the [cyclic grading](fdip-016I)
records how one chosen element acts. Several stabilizer representations
can have the same degree on that element.}

\p{For a massless particle in continuum spacetime, the Lorentz
transformations fixing a reference momentum form its little group.
Weinberg describes the action on states of that momentum in
\citet{§1, Eq. (2), p. 2}{weinberg2020massless}. In the finite-helicity
class he considers, the abelian translations inside the little group
act trivially. In 4 spacetime dimensions the remaining rotations
carry the helicity label
\citet{§1, pp. 2-4}{weinberg2020massless}. The transverse
degrees in \ref{fdip-016J} describe a finite rotation of grid
cochains. They supply no one-particle states or physical helicity.}

\p{Restriction to coarse sites and the resulting changes of labels are treated in [Refining the grid](fdip-01BN).}
